because of its grown-up characters  themes and complex mythology  1999's the mummy provides plenty of  moments that adults will be thinking  about  long after the kids in the room have  moved on to the next thing  in the film's prologue the magi warrior  artist bae  explains that anak su namoon was the  pharaoh's mistress and it was forbidden  for any other man to touch her  to make this point anaksu namoon wore  very little clothes  and was instead covered in an elaborate  body paint design that ran over most of  her body  when her secret lover the high priest  imotep touched her in a moment of  passion  he smeared the paint it's that smudge  that reveals her betrayal to the pharaoh  the smudged body paint is a clever  shorthand that lets the audience in on  the moment without over explaining  it but any adult who's ever put on  makeup body paint or even a halloween  costume  has to wonder why that's the look the  pharaoh settled on it certainly let him  know that someone touched his mistress  but it seems like anaksu namoon could  have just as easily smeared the paint by  bumping into a wall  or laying down for a nap plus waking up  and applying all of that paint  every day must have been the worst  morning routine ever no wonder a naksu  namoon went looking for another man  after the murder of the pharaoh in enoch  su namoon's death  imotep launched a plan to resurrect his  beloved that involved a forbidden ritual  at hammonoptra the city of the dead  imotep dared the god's anger by going  deep into the city  where he took the black book of the dead  from its holy resting place  unfortunately the pharaoh's bodyguards  followed imotep  captured him and subjected him to the  humdai a curse  so cruel that the egyptians had never  performed it before  after being mummified alive and entombed  in a sarcophagus filled with scarab  beetles  cursed to remain the undead for all of  eternity imotep's body was buried in  hamanoptra  then a group of warriors called the magi  begin their millennia-long  effort to watch over the city they knew  that if the high priest ever returned he  would become an all-powerful being that  could destroy the world  it begs the question why did the ancient  egyptians need to use that particular  curse if it had such potentially  devastating consequences  why not just mummify him alive like they  did with the rest of his priests  it makes sense that imotep be punished  for his crimes but this insane curse  feels like an unnecessary risk  evelyn carnahan and her brother jonathan  are the descendants of great explorers  and their adventure begins when jonathan  unknowingly steals the map to  hammonoptra from explorer rick o'connell  while investigating it evie's boss burns  a part of the map  this convinces evie that they need  rick's guidance if they ever hope to  find the lost city  she decides to spring rick from prison  with a little bit of bribery aimed at  the warden  if you cut him down we will give you 10  50 20 430  25 once released the small band of  explorers head off in a race against a  group of treasure hunters who also want  to discover the hidden egyptian city of  the dead  the journey is more than a bit bumpy  after the riverboat catches fire and  sinks  the gang takes the camels to make the  rest of the trip eventually they find  themselves on a desert plain  looking at the sunrise it's then that  rick informs the group that they have to  wait for the sun to hit an exact point  in the sky  before the location of hamanoptra is  revealed to them the treasure hunters  have caught up to them at this point  and as the city is revealed it becomes a  race to see which group reaches their  destination first  the problem here is that rick shouldn't  need the sun to show him the way to  haminoptra  he's been there before after all if he  knew where to stand for the sun to  reveal the city  there's a good chance he could have  navigated the rest of the way simply by  memory  it would have removed the need for a  race but then again the race is a pretty  great set piece for the movie  over the course of the mummy the film  lays out an elaborate mythology for the  fabled city of hamanoptra  though many believe it's only a myth  various scholars have done research on  it and the film establishes clearly that  the city is a very real and very haunted  place  in fact the magi have spent millennia  guarding it and trying to keep it secret  despite the fact that not everyone in  the movie believes in hamanoptra  a lot of people still seem to have a  fairly easy time getting to it  a massive battle erupts amongst its  ruins near the beginning of the movie  and two expeditions travel back to its  location years later  evie talks frequently about its layout  and rick notes that tales of hamonoptra  are so pervasive that his military unit  defied orders to march to egypt from  libya to look for it  so while the medjay are rather reactive  once people make it to hamanoptra  maybe they should start branching out  into more proactive efforts to keep  legends of the city a little more tamped  down  it might make their job easier though it  draws inspiration from historical  concepts like mummies and the egyptian  concept of the afterlife  and it also mentions historical figures  like seti the first  the mummy is firmly based in the world  of fiction it's a big  fun fantasy horror adventure which  allows it to take a lot of liberties  even with biblical history one of the  warnings given by the magi about the  horrible fate that would befall the  world should imotep rise from the dead  is that he could bring the return of the  plagues to  egypt and sure enough as soon as he's  back things like swarms of locusts and  fountains of blood start to plague the  world once more  despite imotep referencing the language  of the slaves when he hears benny  speaking hebrew there aren't any  biblical references in the movie  yet the plagues in question are  specifically drawn from the book of  exodus  so why does imotep get to use the  plagues that god devised to torment the  egyptians  it's probably because these days the  plagues are more associated with egypt  in general  as opposed to the specific story of  moses freeing his people from the  oppression of the pharaoh  the plot of the mummy requires some very  specific events to take place for it to  make sense  some of these events rely on ancient  egyptian rituals that many of the  characters must discover and then  perform correctly  in fact the climax of the movie involves  evie rick  and jonathan battling the all-powerful  imotep and relies on the recital of  spells from a pair of mystical books  the book of the dead contains spells  needed to resurrect people  and the book of ammun rock contains the  spells that allow someone to command the  dead  or render the undead mortal once again  both of these ancient tomes are  presented as extremely dangerous in the  wrong hands  and they even require a special key that  unlocks the books  and allows them to be opened  unfortunately locking the books doesn't  seem to prevent some characters from  using them  near the end of the film jonathan gets  hold of the book of ahmanra  and manages to command a group of mummy  soldiers by reading an  inscription on the cover apparently you  don't need to understand what you're  reading to make the spells work  which increases the danger posed by them  combined with the fact that some of the  spells are written right on the cover of  the books  it gives the impression that the  egyptians who wrote these volumes  probably should have taken a little more  care before publishing  or at the very least devised a more  foolproof security system  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite monster movies are coming  soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one

